
**Slide 1 — Title**
Introduce the project. Say you're comparing five algorithms that all solve the same problem — Maximum Subarray Sum — but with wildly different efficiencies. The goal is to see how algorithmic design choices translate into real, measurable performance differences.

---

**Slide 2 — The Problem**
Explain what the problem is asking: given an array that can have both positive and negative numbers, find the contiguous subarray with the largest possible sum. Walk through the example — point out that the answer isn't the whole array, it's specifically [4, -1, 2, 1] which gives sum = 6. Then briefly mention why this matters in the real world — stock profit windows, signal peaks, DNA scoring, image region detection.

---

**Slide 3 — Algorithms at a Glance**
Give a quick overview of all five algorithms before diving into any one of them. Point out that each one represents a different design paradigm — exhaustive search, precomputation, divide & conquer, and dynamic programming. Note that complexity ranges from O(n³) all the way down to O(n), and highlight the Kadane row as the theoretically optimal solution.

---

**Slide 4 — Pseudocode Part I**
Walk through each of the four pseudocodes briefly. For Brute Force — three nested loops, check every possible subarray. For Better Brute Force — eliminate the innermost loop by accumulating the sum as you go. For Prefix Sum — precompute cumulative sums so any subarray sum is just a subtraction, but you still need two loops to check all pairs. For Kadane — the key insight is the decision at each element: is it better to extend the current subarray or start fresh? That single decision makes it O(n).

---

**Slide 5 — Pseudocode: Divide & Conquer**
Explain the three-step structure. Divide: split the array at the midpoint. Conquer: recursively find the max subarray in the left half and right half. Combine: find the max subarray that crosses the midpoint — this is done by expanding outward from the middle in both directions. The final answer is the best of those three. The recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n) resolves to O(n log n) by the Master Theorem.

---

**Slide 6 — Theoretical Analysis**
Show how dramatically the operation counts diverge as n grows. At n=1,000, Brute Force needs a billion operations while Kadane needs just 1,000. The line chart makes this visual — the curves fan out quickly. Emphasize that this isn't just a constant-factor difference, it's a fundamentally different growth rate.

---

**Slide 7 — Empirical Results (Log-Log Plot)**
This is the key evidence slide. Explain that a log-log plot turns exponential curves into straight lines — the slope of each line equals the exponent in the complexity. Point out that Brute Force has a steep slope around 3, the quadratic algorithms have slope ~2, D&C is between 1 and 2, and Kadane is nearly flat. The fact that the slopes match the theory is the main finding — your implementations are correct and behave as expected.

---

**Slide 8 — Speedup Chart**
Put the performance gap in concrete terms. At n=3,000, Kadane is roughly half a million times faster than Brute Force. Even D&C is about 27,000 times faster. The speedup keeps growing as n increases because the gap between complexity classes widens. This slide drives home why algorithm choice matters far more than hardware or micro-optimizations.

---

**Slide 9 — Runtime at n=3000**
Give the actual measured numbers. Brute Force took over 13 seconds. Kadane took 0.025 milliseconds. Reference the bar chart and note that on a log scale, Brute Force's bars dwarf everything else — Better BF and Prefix Sum are visually close to each other, and D&C and Kadane are nearly invisible at the bottom. Also note that all algorithms got the same correct answer across all datasets.

