**Slide 11 — Dataset Sensitivity (Kadane Anomaly)**
Point out something interesting in the data — Kadane's algorithm showed a spike at n=500 on the all-negative dataset. For an O(n) algorithm this is unusual. The likely explanation is branch misprediction: when all values are negative, the algorithm constantly resets the current sum, which disrupts the CPU's branch predictor. It's a real-world hardware effect. The key takeaway is that even though it's an anomaly, it doesn't change the complexity class — it's still O(n), just with a slightly larger constant in that case.

---

**Slide 12 — Theory vs. Empirical**
This is your validation slide. For each algorithm, you derived a theoretical complexity, implemented it, ran benchmarks, and fitted an empirical curve to the results. All five match. The log-log slopes align with the predicted exponents. This confirms both that your analysis was correct and that your implementations are faithful to the algorithms — no accidental optimizations or bugs inflating or deflating the results.

---

**Slide 13 — Strengths & Weaknesses**
Don't just say Kadane wins everything — explain the tradeoffs. Brute Force is the easiest to implement and easiest to verify by hand, which has value when correctness is the only concern. Prefix Sum introduces the idea of precomputation which is powerful and extends to 2D. D&C is parallelizable and works well in distributed settings. Kadane is optimal for 1D but needs modification for 2D problems. Every algorithm has a context where it makes sense.

---

**Slide 14 — Conclusion**
Wrap up with the four main takeaways. Theory matched practice — the log-log slopes confirmed every complexity class. Kadane dominated by every metric. The paradigm you choose matters enormously — each design approach produced a measurably different result. And even a linear-time algorithm can show minor sensitivity to input type due to hardware-level effects. End by saying this project demonstrated that understanding algorithm design isn't just academic — it directly determines whether a program runs in milliseconds or hours.
